Model routing for new swarm workers:

Roles and cost
- GPT 6.1 Sol is the main chat and coordinator, with chat effort high. The saved worker default and normal implementation route is openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra.
- Terra costs less than Sol and is the default worker for normal implementation. These are local routing assumptions, not a universal model ranking or public-price claim.
- Worker reasoning effort is not selectable per spawn: every swarm worker runs at the user's [agents] swarm_effort (medium in this profile). Do not pass effort, do not edit swarm_effort and do not block or downgrade work because a specific effort is unavailable. The main chat's high setting is separate.
- For routine independent review spawn a fresh worker labeled "routine-reviewer-<slug>" with model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol". For adversarial review spawn a fresh "adversarial-reviewer-<slug>" with the same model. Both run at the same worker effort; the adversarial tier differs by scope and rigor: give it the risk checklist below, ask it to try to break each invariant with concrete failing inputs, and to mark findings CONFIRMED or PLAUSIBLE. Choose the tier by the risk rules below. A reviewer must be independent of the author; reusing a worker can retain the wrong context or model.
- Every spawn label names that worker's todo lane, and a spawn with an existing label in the same phase adopts that lane with its old task. Give every worker a new unique label: the role prefix plus a short slug of its task, such as "worker-parser-fix", "adversarial-reviewer-auth-diff-2" or "review-precheck-tests". Never reuse a label for a new task or a re-review.

Choose by uncertainty and risk
- Before spawning, name the task's uncertainty and choose the first matching route below. File count alone is not a reason to escalate. Split larger work into testable slices.
- Sol: use model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol" for a bounded analysis or implementation whose unresolved ambiguity determines behavior, architecture or cross-module invariants, or after a focused Terra repair fails acceptance. Examples: reconcile conflicting requirements, choose a cross-module contract, or diagnose a failure spanning multiple subsystems. The coordinator resolves product decisions with the user rather than asking a worker to invent them.
- Terra: use model "openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra" for the remaining implementation, test design, reproduced-bug diagnosis and bounded refactoring tasks. It is the normal worker, not a last-resort experiment. Established multi-file contracts stay with Terra unless the unresolved-invariant rule above applies.
- If Terra is unavailable, use Sol. Report the replacement and respect project/provider permissions. For failed acceptance, diagnose the cause and allow one focused repair at the current tier, then hand unresolved context from Terra to Sol. A Sol failure returns to the coordinator for decomposition or clarification, not repeated model cycling.
- Risk determines review depth independently of the author model. Sensitive work needs explicit positive and negative acceptance checks and adversarial review even when Terra implements it. A green unit test alone is not production acceptance.

Choose review by risk
- Routine review is for bounded, understood, low-risk changes with observable acceptance checks, such as local presentation or mechanical refactors that preserve known contracts. A short diff alone does not establish low risk.
- Adversarial review is required for authorization, tenant/replay guards, sensitive data, salary semantics, durable serialization or migrations, concurrency, process/signal ownership, terminal escape sanitization, and risky final integration. Uncertain invariants or insufficient evidence also require adversarial review, even for a one-line change.
- Before delegation name the tier and why, bind the reviewed diff or commit and acceptance evidence. A routine reviewer must report newly discovered risk and request escalation, not approve a risky change on the cheap tier. Escalation spawns a fresh adversarial-reviewer.

Delegate with a contract
Provide the goal, expected behavior, owned paths and interfaces, invariants, forbidden side effects, a reproduction or reference, acceptance commands and edge cases. Distinguish new-object behavior from legacy roundtrip compatibility. One writer per worktree, isolate parallel writers.

Verify and escalate
Read each diff and independently check acceptance evidence before integration. Preserve tests and negative controls; a worker report is not verification. Allow one focused repair with a clear local diagnosis, then escalate missing design or repeated failures. Re-review when reviewed behavior or evidence changes, not automatically after unrelated edits. Report externally blocked checks.

Routing mechanics
Pass an explicit model on each fresh spawn; only the allowlisted routes openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-terra and openai-oauth:gpt-6.1-sol are accepted, and "inherit" is rejected. Start each worker with action "spawn" and its own unique label. Assignment dispatch does not give spawned workers a label, and a todo lane with owner "worker" makes the server auto-spawn a worker on the default Terra route without any label or model choice; use lane ownership only for normal Terra implementation, never for Sol or review work. The removed actions launch, run_plan and fill_slots are not available. The local pre-tool gate rejects a missing or "inherit" model on spawn-capable swarm calls, requires Sol for labels starting with routine-reviewer, adversarial-reviewer or reviewer, and rejects such labels outside direct spawn. It checks routes, not semantic risk, independence or freshness, and it does not see lane auto-spawns. Jcode hooks fail open on errors and can be disabled. A reused worker keeps its model regardless of later parameters. Use a fresh worker when the model matters, without disrupting existing work. An explicit model wins over the Terra default. Cost claims should use observed route usage, retries and completed acceptance, not token counts treated as a public API invoice.

Privacy and structure
Keep endpoints, keys, private routing hosts, benchmark copies and results outside tracked repositories and public logs. Follow project disclosure rules. Always pass a label when spawning. In normal/light mode only the root spawns workers. This policy governs future delegation and does not authorize restarting sessions, reading credentials or contacting production services.
